Track 2: Brass Thimbles Bleed into the Rails
- Story & Context: Huddled beside towering canvas sacks in the luggage carriage, disgraced switchman Arthur Vance wept into his pocket sampler as he plugged 1960s vacuum tubes directly into the train’s battery bay, confessing to months of sabotaging rail signals just to buy antique mallet instruments. He clattered vintage brass thimbles over his fingertips like mechanical spiders across his wooden xylophone, weaving manic polyrhythmic clicks into an oceanic, half-time future bass drop. When transit inspectors broke the latch and pointed halogen beams into his eyes, Arthur pressed his forehead to the vibrating steel wall, refusing to halt the blooming tape-flanged chords that rattled his ribcage. Tears spattered his battery dock as his trembling falsetto pierced the deep, submerged Reese bass: "I sold our morning tickets for a spool of silver wire."
